WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.979
Imagine a camera with only two pixels.

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.086
Every picture it takes is

3
00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:10.030
two brightness numbers

4
00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.704
so make it a point in the plane

5
00:00:12.704 --> 00:00:15.540
left pixel across, right pixel up.

6
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.767
A blur filter mixes the two pixels

7
00:00:18.849 --> 00:00:22.301
each new pixel is three quarters of itself

8
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:25.013
plus a quarter of its neighbour.

9
00:00:25.013 --> 00:00:27.890
Here are three pictures as arrows.

10
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.331
Now blur the whole plane.

11
00:00:30.331 --> 00:00:33.258
Every arrow gets a little shorter

12
00:00:33.342 --> 00:00:35.600
and every one of them turns

13
00:00:35.684 --> 00:00:37.440
towards the diagonal.

14
00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.777
Is there any picture whose arrow

15
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.830
does not turn at all?

16
00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.070
Pause and look for one.

17
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.713
Start again, and draw two lines first

18
00:00:51.713 --> 00:00:55.180
the diagonal, where both pixels are equal

19
00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.115
and the other diagonal

20
00:00:57.115 --> 00:00:59.130
where they are opposite.

21
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.209
On the first line put even grey

22
00:01:02.209 --> 00:01:03.318
one and one.

23
00:01:03.318 --> 00:01:05.621
On the second put an edge

24
00:01:05.621 --> 00:01:07.242
one and minus one.

25
00:01:07.242 --> 00:01:09.886
It is not a picture by itself

26
00:01:09.971 --> 00:01:12.956
it is the part that makes one pixel

27
00:01:13.041 --> 00:01:15.600
brighter and the other darker.

28
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.565
Add the picture three and one

29
00:01:18.565 --> 00:01:21.790
and pin down two points of its line.

30
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.213
Blur the plane once more.

31
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:26.949
Grey comes out exactly the same.

32
00:01:26.949 --> 00:01:29.602
The edge stays on its own line

33
00:01:29.602 --> 00:01:31.260
at half its length.

34
00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:35.633
But three and one becomes two and a half

35
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:39.362
one and a half: it has left its line.

36
00:01:39.362 --> 00:01:42.502
The blur has two special lines

37
00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.250
and on them it only scales.

38
00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:49.246
These special directions have a name.

39
00:01:49.339 --> 00:01:53.124
A nonzero vector v is an eigenvector of A

40
00:01:53.217 --> 00:01:56.910
when A times v is just a number times v.

41
00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.599
That number, lambda, is the eigenvalue

42
00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:04.024
how much the line is stretched, shrunk

43
00:02:04.024 --> 00:02:05.051
or flipped.

44
00:02:05.051 --> 00:02:08.818
For the blur, grey has lambda equal to one

45
00:02:08.818 --> 00:02:12.500
and the edge has lambda equal to one half.

46
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.588
Any multiple of an eigenvector

47
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:16.957
works as well.

48
00:02:16.957 --> 00:02:20.791
Two, two is still grey, and it stays put.

49
00:02:20.791 --> 00:02:23.803
Minus two, two is still an edge

50
00:02:23.803 --> 00:02:26.450
and one more blur halves it.

51
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.691
So the eigenvector is really the whole line

52
00:02:30.691 --> 00:02:33.520
not one particular arrow on it.

53
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.356
Here is a tempting thought

54
00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:40.034
the zero vector must be an eigenvector too

55
00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:43.460
since A times zero is lambda times zero.

56
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.597
The trouble is that it holds

57
00:02:46.688 --> 00:02:50.341
for every lambda at once: one, one half

58
00:02:50.341 --> 00:02:52.168
seven, minus three.

59
00:02:52.168 --> 00:02:53.720
If zero counted

60
00:02:53.811 --> 00:02:57.008
every number would be an eigenvalue

61
00:02:57.099 --> 00:02:58.560
of every matrix.

62
00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.660
So eigenvectors must be nonzero.

63
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.840
The eigenvalue zero is fine, though

64
00:03:04.926 --> 00:03:07.504
it means the matrix flattens a

65
00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.910
whole line onto the origin

66
00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:12.230
so the matrix is singular.

67
00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.744
Why do the special lines matter?

68
00:03:15.744 --> 00:03:19.595
Blur the picture three and one, not once

69
00:03:19.595 --> 00:03:20.970
but ten times.

70
00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.887
Without multiplying ten times

71
00:03:23.887 --> 00:03:26.454
can you say where it ends up?

72
00:03:26.454 --> 00:03:28.080
Pause and predict.

73
00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.030
Split it along the two lines.

74
00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:36.727
Three and one is two times grey

75
00:03:36.727 --> 00:03:39.423
plus one times the edge picture.

76
00:03:39.423 --> 00:03:42.202
Two is the average of the pixels

77
00:03:42.202 --> 00:03:44.980
and one is half their difference.

78
00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:46.215
Now blur

79
00:03:46.215 --> 00:03:50.249
the grey part stays exactly where it is

80
00:03:50.249 --> 00:03:53.594
and the edge part is cut in half.

81
00:03:53.594 --> 00:03:56.250
Blur again, and once more.

82
00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.136
Two and a half, then two and a quarter

83
00:04:00.136 --> 00:04:02.312
then two and an eighth.

84
00:04:02.312 --> 00:04:04.669
After k blurs: two greys

85
00:04:04.669 --> 00:04:08.387
plus one half to the power k of an edge.

86
00:04:08.387 --> 00:04:11.923
After ten, two point zero zero one and

87
00:04:12.013 --> 00:04:14.280
one point nine nine nine.

88
00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:17.895
The average survives forever.

89
00:04:17.895 --> 00:04:20.710
The contrast fades away.

90
00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.558
Now turn the filter around.

91
00:04:23.642 --> 00:04:25.658
A sharpen filter has the

92
00:04:25.742 --> 00:04:27.674
same two special lines.

93
00:04:27.674 --> 00:04:31.370
Grey still stays put, but the edge doubles.

94
00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:35.469
Sharpen three and one: four and zero.

95
00:04:35.469 --> 00:04:38.103
Again: six and minus two.

96
00:04:38.205 --> 00:04:41.751
After ten rounds the edge has grown

97
00:04:41.852 --> 00:04:45.196
a thousand and twenty-four times

98
00:04:45.196 --> 00:04:48.134
one thousand and twenty-six

99
00:04:48.134 --> 00:04:51.680
minus one thousand and twenty-two.

100
00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:55.241
Repeated averaging is also what a graph neural

101
00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:57.544
network layer does to its nodes.

102
00:04:57.544 --> 00:04:58.870
Stack many layers

103
00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.731
and every node drifts to the same value.

104
00:05:01.731 --> 00:05:03.895
People call it over-smoothing.

105
00:05:03.895 --> 00:05:05.430
It is our blur again.

106
00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.852
So, to sum up: under a matrix

107
00:05:08.852 --> 00:05:10.557
most arrows turn.

108
00:05:10.652 --> 00:05:14.157
An eigenvector is a nonzero direction

109
00:05:14.252 --> 00:05:16.147
that is only scaled

110
00:05:16.147 --> 00:05:18.610
by its eigenvalue lambda.

111
00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.327
Write a vector along the eigenvectors

112
00:05:22.416 --> 00:05:25.163
and powers of the matrix become

113
00:05:25.252 --> 00:05:27.822
powers of numbers: one stays

114
00:05:27.822 --> 00:05:30.480
one half fades, two explodes.

115
00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:33.755
Here we guessed the special lines.

116
00:05:33.755 --> 00:05:37.005
In part two we find them for any matrix

117
00:05:37.005 --> 00:05:39.779
from a single equation for lambda

118
00:05:39.858 --> 00:05:42.632
and meet matrices whose eigenvalues

119
00:05:42.711 --> 00:05:43.900
are a surprise.
